course: dictionary
lesson: 9449
title: One word, many meanings: stand
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word stand can mean different things, so context tells you which meaning you need. Stand can be a verb: «To stand up is to get onto your feet.» It can also be a noun: a «lemonade stand» is a small stall that sells things.
explain: Look at the words around stand. «The house stands on a hill» means the house is in a place. «Stand still» means do not move. Ask: what would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Stand can also mean to mean. In «km» stands for kilometer, the letters show what the short form stands for. «Can't stand» is a feeling clue: it means strongly dislike.
explain: Stand also has a meaning about people. To stand up for someone is to defend them, so if a friend is being picked on, you stand up for them. When the context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Stand is a verb and a noun: stand, stands, stood, standing.
fact: A stand is a small stall that sells things, such as a lemonade stand.
fact: Maths: money = price × cups sold. A lemonade stand that sells 12 cups at 50p each earns 12 × 50p = 600p, which is £6.
fact: «Stand still» means do not move: if a strange dog runs at you, «be a tree», stand still and stay quiet.
fact: «Stand for» means to mean, as in «km» stands for kilometer.
exercise: «The children stood in a line outside the school.» What does stood mean here? | Were sitting down | Were singing loudly | *Were on their feet | Were lost
exercise: Mia's lemonade stand sold 12 cups at 50p each. How much money did she earn? | *£6 | 50p | £2 | £12
exercise: In «km» stands for kilometer, what does stands for mean? | *To mean | To buy | To run | To sit
test: Ella says, «Stand up for your friends when they are picked on.» What does stand up for mean? | Get onto your feet only | Buy a stall | *Defend them | Hate them
test: Ella says, «I can't stand the smell of onions.» What does can't stand mean? | *Strongly dislike | Like it very much | Stand in a line | Stand for a long time
exam: «If a strange dog runs at you, do not run or scream.» What should you do instead? | *Stand still, fold your arms in close and stay quiet | Run and scream | Wave at the dog | Call a friend to fight it
